## Mistake #4: Data Quality Issues Discovered Late

### What Happens:
You build complex analysis over 2-3 weeks. In Week 4, you notice the numbers don't match published reports. You investigate and discover data quality issues. Now you need to fix data issues AND redo analysis.

### Why It's Expensive:
- **Time wasted:** Built complex logic on bad data
- **Emergency mode:** Now rushed to fix issues and deliver on time
- **Quality risk:** More likely to miss other issues when rushing

### Prevention:
✅ **Day 3:** Run data validation checks before building anything complex  
✅ **Validate:** Do totals match published reports?  
✅ **Investigate:** Any unexpected nulls, duplicates, or outliers?  
✅ **Document:** Create data quality log with all issues  

**Don't build a skyscraper on a cracked foundation.**

---

### Real Examples:

**Example 1: The Null Sale Price**
> Analyst built entire lien loss analysis over 2 weeks. In final QA, discovered 30% of records had null sale prices. Had to go back and decide how to handle nulls, recalculate everything.

**Example 2: The Duplicate Records**
> Analyst didn't check for duplicates. Final analysis showed 150% recovery rate (impossible). Spent days debugging. Root cause: source table had duplicate records that needed deduplication.

**Example 3: The Timing Lag**
> Analyst used "real-time" data that actually had a 3-day lag. Numbers didn't match operational reports because they were using different as-of dates. Could have been caught with simple validation query.

---

## Mistake #5: Working in Isolation

### What Happens:
You keep your head down and grind through the analysis. You don't want to bother anyone or show incomplete work. You encounter blockers but try to figure them out alone. You waste days on problems that someone else could have solved in minutes.

### Why It's Expensive:
- **Reinventing the wheel:** Someone else already solved that problem
- **Wrong assumptions:** No one to catch your mistakes
- **Missed context:** Don't learn about important business considerations

### Prevention:
✅ **Check-ins:** Regular stakeholder meetings (even if brief)  
✅ **Collaborate:** Ask colleagues who've done similar work  
✅ **Show work:** Get feedback on drafts, even rough ones  
✅ **Ask questions:** Don't spin alone for more than 2 hours  

---

### Real Examples:

**Example 1: The Solved Problem**
> Analyst spent 3 days figuring out how to join two tables with many-to-many relationship. A colleague had already built the logic for a previous project and shared it in 10 minutes.

**Example 2: The Business Rule**
> Analyst made assumptions about how to handle a specific account type. Built entire analysis around those assumptions. Subject matter expert could have clarified in 5 minutes that the assumptions were wrong.

**Example 3: The Data Source**
> Analyst struggled for days to extract data from one system. Didn't know that a cleaner, more complete version of the data existed in a different system that the team regularly used.

---

## Mistake #6: No Mockup of Final Deliverable

### What Happens:
You have a general idea of what the deliverable should look like, but never explicitly sketch it out. You build the analysis and then figure out how to present it. The final format ends up being different than what stakeholder expected.

### Why It's Expensive:
- **Format rework:** Built in Excel, needed PowerPoint (or vice versa)
- **Content rework:** Wrong level of detail or wrong breakdowns
- **Structure rework:** Organized by one dimension, needed another

### Prevention:
✅ **Week 1:** Create a mockup (even rough sketch)  
✅ **Get approval:** Show stakeholder the structure before building  
✅ **Use as guide:** Build directly to the mockup format  

**"If I don't know what success looks like, how will I know when I've achieved it?"**

---

### Real Examples:

**Example 1: Excel vs PowerPoint**
> Analyst assumed stakeholder wanted detailed Excel workbook with multiple tabs and formulas. Stakeholder needed executive-level PowerPoint with 5 summary slides. Spent days converting between formats.

**Example 2: Detail Level Mismatch**
> Analyst built very granular analysis (daily data, 50+ categories). Stakeholder wanted high-level trends (monthly data, top 10 categories). Most of the detailed work wasn't needed.

**Example 3: Wrong Aggregation**
> Analyst organized deliverable by product line (because that's how data was structured). Stakeholder needed it organized by region (because that's how they make decisions). Required complete reorganization.

---

## Mistake #7: "I'll Document This Later"

### What Happens:
You're focused on building the analysis. Documentation seems like busy work. You think "I'll remember how this works" or "I'll document it after delivery." You never do. Six months later (or even 6 weeks), you or someone else needs to update the analysis and can't figure out how it works.

### Why It's Expensive:
- **Reverse engineering:** Spending hours figuring out your own code
- **Maintenance nightmare:** Can't confidently update or fix issues
- **Knowledge loss:** If you leave, no one can maintain it

### Prevention:
✅ **Document as you build,** not after  
✅ **Comment your code:** Explain WHY, not just WHAT  
✅ **Write README:** Document purpose, data sources, how to run  
✅ **Create runbook:** If recurring, document the process  

**"The best time to document is now. The second best time is never going to happen."**

---

### Real Examples:

**Example 1: The Mystery Formula**
> Analyst created complex Excel formula to calculate adjusted recovery rate. Didn't document the logic. Six months later, stakeholder asked for update. Analyst had forgotten why certain adjustments were made. Spent days reconstructing the logic.

**Example 2: The Undocumented Pipeline**
> Analyst built Python script with 500 lines of data transformations. No comments, no documentation. When data source changed format, no one could figure out which transformations were affected. Had to rebuild from scratch.

**Example 3: The Lost Knowledge**
> Analyst completed analysis and moved to different team. Stakeholder requested update 3 months later. New analyst couldn't figure out the process, had to ask multiple people, still got it wrong. Original analyst had to come back and fix it.

---

## Mistake #8: Scope Creep Without Timeline Adjustment

### What Happens:
Stakeholder asks for "one more thing" in Week 2. Then another thing in Week 3. You say yes to keep them happy. Original 4-week project is now 6-8 weeks, but deadline hasn't changed. You're stressed, working overtime, and quality suffers.

### Why It's Expensive:
- **Burnout:** Unsustainable pace
- **Quality issues:** Rushing leads to mistakes
- **Missed deadline:** Can't deliver everything on time

### Prevention:
✅ **Assess impact:** "This would add X hours of work"  
✅ **Force trade-offs:** "We can add this, but need to cut that OR extend timeline"  
✅ **Update charter:** Document scope changes and get re-approval  
✅ **Learn to say no:** "That's a great idea for Phase 2"  

---

### Real Examples:

**Example 1: The Growing List**
> Week 1: Add regional breakdown  
> Week 2: Add vendor comparison  
> Week 3: Add historical trend  
> Week 4: Add predictive forecast  
>
> Original scope was simple performance analysis. Grew into comprehensive study. Timeline stayed 4 weeks. Analyst worked nights and weekends. Final product was rushed and had errors.

**Example 2: The "Quick Add"**
> Stakeholder: "Can you just quickly add..." (5 times)  
> Each "quick add" was 2-4 hours  
> Analyst didn't push back  
> Project took 3 extra weeks  

**Example 3: The Perfectionist Stakeholder**
> Every review: "Can we adjust the colors? Can we add more detail here? Can we try a different chart?"  
> Analyst kept iterating  
> Never shipped the analysis  
> Eventually stakeholder made decision without it  

---

## Mistake #9: Analysis Without Recommendations

### What Happens:
You build thorough analysis, create beautiful visualizations, present all the data. You end with "So... that's what the data shows." Stakeholder asks "So what should I do?" You haven't thought about the action items.

### Why It's Expensive:
- **Missed value:** Analysis that doesn't drive action is wasted
- **Second meeting needed:** Have to reconvene to discuss recommendations
- **Stakeholder frustration:** Expected insights, got data dump

### Prevention:
✅ **Start with business question:** What decision is this informing?  
✅ **BLUF approach:** Bottom line up front  
✅ **So what? Now what?:** Translate findings to action  
✅ **Prioritize recommendations:** High impact, low effort first  

**"Data isn't insights. Insights aren't recommendations. Recommendations drive action."**

---

### Real Examples:

**Example 1: The Data Dump**
> Analyst: "Vendor A has 75% recovery rate, Vendor B has 68%, Vendor C has 71%"
> Stakeholder: "Okay... so what should I do?"
> Analyst: "Um... I guess use Vendor A more?"
>
> Better approach: "Recommend increasing Vendor A allocation by 15% and decreasing Vendor C by 15%. Expected impact: $250K improvement in recovery."

**Example 2: The Observation Without Action**
> Analyst: "We see recovery rates declining over the past 6 months"
> Stakeholder: "Why? What should we do about it?"
> Analyst: "I don't know, I just analyzed the trend"
>
> Better approach: "Recovery rates declined 8% due to: (1) higher loan balances, (2) lower vehicle values. Recommend: (1) adjust loan-to-value ratios, (2) evaluate pricing model."

---

## Mistake #10: Perfectionism Over Pragmatism

### What Happens:
You keep refining, adjusting, perfecting. You're not satisfied with "good enough." You want the analysis to be bulletproof. You spend 80 hours to improve from 90% to 95% quality. Meanwhile, the business decision is delayed waiting for your perfect analysis.

### Why It's Expensive:
- **Diminishing returns:** Last 10% of polish takes 50% of time
- **Opportunity cost:** Could have moved on to next project
- **Delayed decisions:** Perfect analysis that arrives too late doesn't help

### Prevention:
✅ **Define "done" upfront:** What's the acceptance criteria?  
✅ **80/20 rule:** Deliver 80% of value quickly  
✅ **Ship and iterate:** Get feedback on MVP, enhance later  
✅ **Ask:** "Is this good enough to make the decision?"  

**"The best analysis gets used, not the one that's perfect."**

---

### Real Examples:

**Example 1: The Endless Refinement**
> Analyst kept tweaking chart colors, fonts, layouts  
> Spent 8 hours on formatting  
> Stakeholder didn't care about perfect formatting  
> Just needed the numbers to make a decision  

**Example 2: The Over-Engineered Solution**
> Analyst built sophisticated machine learning model  
> Took 6 weeks to develop  
> Simple regression model would have given 90% of the insight in 3 days  
> By the time ML model was done, business had moved on  

**Example 3: The Premature Optimization**
> Analyst optimized code to run 10 seconds faster  
> Spent 4 hours on optimization  
> Code only runs once per month  
> Saved 2 minutes per year, spent 4 hours optimizing  

